<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE concept PUBLIC "-//OASIS//DTD DITA Concept//EN" "concept.dtd">
<!-- CONCEPT TEMPLATE (authoring standards, section 5). A concept explains an idea. It holds no
     procedure: a task does that. Copy this file, then:
       1. Name the file for a noun phrase: workspaces.dita. Make the id the same.
       2. Title: a noun phrase in sentence case. Short description: one sentence.
       3. Open with one paragraph that says what the idea is and why the reader cares.
       4. Use titled sections. Aim for 500 words or fewer. Split anything longer.
       5. Explain each hard word once, in plain words, and link it to its glossary entry on first use
          with <term keyref="term-..."/>.
       6. A diagram needs a title, a description, and a text list of the same links (see the diagram
          template). Never put information only in a picture.
       7. Related links come from the relationship table in the chapter map. Do not write them here.
     A concept is linked from the tasks that need it. It is never in the way of them. -->
<concept id="template-concept" xml:lang="en-GB">
  <title>Things</title>
  <shortdesc>What a thing is, and why it helps you keep your family history in order.</shortdesc>
  <prolog>
    <metadata>
      <audience type="user" experiencelevel="novice"/>
      <category>Concept</category>
      <keywords>
        <keyword>idea</keyword>
      </keywords>
    </metadata>
  </prolog>
  <conbody>
    <p>Say what the idea is in one sentence. Then say why it matters to the reader.</p>
    <section>
      <title>What it looks like</title>
      <p>Describe what the reader sees, with the label from the app by key: <uicontrol keyref="ui-facts"/>.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <title>Why it helps</title>
      <p>Give one reason, with one example from the Brontë dataset.</p>
    </section>
  </conbody>
</concept>
